// src/pages.js
var PAGES = { "supplier-statement-check": { "title": "MCP Supplier Statement Check - Supplied AP Open-Item Evidence", "tagline": "Compare supplied supplier statement and AP ledger open items, with complete exact-key evidence.", "description": "Compare two supplied open-item lists by exact entity, supplier, currency, invoice or credit-note type and reference. Retain original records for agreement, missing-side items, amount/date differences and repeated-reference ambiguity. No balances, payments, PDF/OCR or ERP access.", "html": '<h1>Supplier statement reconciliation MCP server</h1>\n<p>Compare a supplied supplier statement with an accounts-payable open-item export.\nFind invoices or credit notes present on only one side, conflicting outstanding\namounts or dates, and repeated references that cannot be paired safely. This local\nMCP server preserves every original row and explains each finding. It checks two\nsupplied open-item lists; it does not reconcile opening/closing balances, match\npayments, connect to an ERP, read PDFs, or approve payment.</p>\n<p>Built by <a href="https://github.com/theluckystrike">theluckystrike</a>. MIT. Support: <a href="mailto:support@zovo.one">support@zovo.one</a>.</p>\n<h2>Try it before installing</h2>\n<p><a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/guides/compare-supplier-statement-ap-open-items-in-claude#supplier-demo">Run the free supplier statement example in your browser</a>.\nUse the synthetic example to inspect missing items and amount/date discrepancies.\nThe browser proof and this server use the same comparison core. No account or API\nkey is needed for the local server&#39;s free tier.\n<a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/s/supplier-statement-check">Product and installation options</a>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Your task</th>\n<th>Start here</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Compare supplier/vendor statement open items with an AP open-item export</td>\n<td>This server: <code>supplier_statement_check</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Find repeated invoice references across supplied invoice history</td>\n<td><a href="https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-duplicate-invoice-check">Duplicate supplier invoice review</a>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Check invoice lines against a purchase order and accepted receipts</td>\n<td><a href="https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-three-way-match">Three-way invoice matching</a>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>These comparisons require the relevant source data. They do not retrieve missing\ndocuments or authorize a payment. Full diagnostics and CSV remain free; the paid\nupgrade removes only the limit on saved report count.</p>\n<h2>Install</h2>\n<p>Requires Node.js 18 or newer and an MCP client. Claude Desktop can open the\n<a href="https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-servers/releases/download/v0.25.0/supplier-statement-check.mcpb">supplier-statement-check.mcpb v0.25.0 package</a>.\nOther clients can build the source below. The npm package is not published;\n<code>npx @theluckystrike/mcp-supplier-statement-check</code> is not an installation route.</p>\n<pre><code class="language-sh">git clone https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-servers.git\ncd mcp-servers\nnpm install\nnode scripts/bootstrap-timezone.mjs\nnpm run build --workspace @theluckystrike/mcp-license\nnpm run build --workspace @theluckystrike/mcp-supplier-statement-check\n</code></pre>\n<p>These commands build only the shared license dependency and this server. Replace\n<code>/absolute/path</code> below with the source checkout location. If your MCP client cannot\nfind <code>node</code>, use its full executable path.</p>\n<p>Claude Desktop: merge into <code>mcpServers</code> in\n<code>~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json</code> on macOS, or\n<code>%APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json</code> on Windows:</p>\n<pre><code class="language-json">{&quot;mcpServers&quot;:{&quot;supplier-statement-check&quot;:{&quot;command&quot;:&quot;node&quot;,&quot;args&quot;:[&quot;/absolute/path/mcp-servers/servers/supplier-statement-check/dist/index.js&quot;]}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Claude Code:</p>\n<pre><code class="language-sh">claude mcp add supplier-statement-check -- node /absolute/path/mcp-servers/servers/supplier-statement-check/dist/index.js\n</code></pre>\n<p>Cursor: merge into <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code> in the project or <code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class="language-json">{&quot;mcpServers&quot;:{&quot;supplier-statement-check&quot;:{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;stdio&quot;,&quot;command&quot;:&quot;node&quot;,&quot;args&quot;:[&quot;/absolute/path/mcp-servers/servers/supplier-statement-check/dist/index.js&quot;]}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>VS Code: put the entry under <code>servers</code> in <code>.vscode/mcp.json</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class="language-json">{&quot;servers&quot;:{&quot;supplier-statement-check&quot;:{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;stdio&quot;,&quot;command&quot;:&quot;node&quot;,&quot;args&quot;:[&quot;/absolute/path/mcp-servers/servers/supplier-statement-check/dist/index.js&quot;]}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Windsurf&#39;s legacy Cascade agent: use the Claude Desktop <code>mcpServers</code> entry in\n<code>~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json</code>. That file applies to Cascade; a different\nagent can use a different configuration route.</p>\n<p>Cline: open MCP Servers, Configure, Configure MCP Servers, and add:</p>\n<pre><code class="language-json">{&quot;mcpServers&quot;:{&quot;supplier-statement-check&quot;:{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;stdio&quot;,&quot;command&quot;:&quot;node&quot;,&quot;args&quot;:[&quot;/absolute/path/mcp-servers/servers/supplier-statement-check/dist/index.js&quot;],&quot;disabled&quot;:false,&quot;autoApprove&quot;:[]}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>These are local stdio configurations. This server has no hosted endpoint or browser\nchat connector. Reconnect your client, call <code>license_status</code>, then run the example\nbelow. Tool discovery alone does not verify a comparison.</p>\n<h2>Tools</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Input and result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>supplier_statement_check</code></td>\n<td><code>as_of</code>, <code>statement_records</code>, <code>ledger_records</code>, optional <code>save:false</code>; complete evidence or a paginated overview with a free preview ID.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>report_list</code></td>\n<td>Optional <code>offset:0</code>, <code>limit:20</code> (max 100); saved UUIDs and count summaries.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>report_get</code></td>\n<td>Saved <code>id</code>; validated source rows and recomputed comparison.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>report_page</code></td>\n<td>Saved/preview <code>id</code>, <code>section</code>, optional <code>offset</code>, <code>limit</code> (max 100) and <code>group_id</code>; all source evidence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>report_delete</code></td>\n<td>Saved <code>id</code>; delete one local report and free a saved slot.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>report_export_csv</code></td>\n<td>Saved/preview <code>id</code>, optional <code>offset</code>, <code>limit</code> (max 2000); CSV with one row per original source item.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>license_status</code></td>\n<td>No arguments; current offline tier and upgrade link.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>license_activate</code></td>\n<td><code>key</code>; verify and save an offline Pro license.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>A missing item and a conflicting amount</h2>\n<p>Call <code>supplier_statement_check</code> with these synthetic inputs:</p>\n<pre><code class="language-json">{\n  &quot;as_of&quot;: &quot;2026-10-26&quot;,\n  &quot;statement_records&quot;: [\n    {&quot;record_id&quot;:&quot;S1&quot;,&quot;entity_id&quot;:&quot;UK-1&quot;,&quot;supplier_id&quot;:&quot;V-9&quot;,&quot;currency&quot;:&quot;USD&quot;,&quot;document_type&quot;:&quot;invoice&quot;,&quot;document_reference&quot;:&quot;INV-001&quot;,&quot;document_date&quot;:&quot;2026-10-01&quot;,&quot;outstanding_minor&quot;:12500},\n    {&quot;record_id&quot;:&quot;S2&quot;,&quot;entity_id&quot;:&quot;UK-1&quot;,&quot;supplier_id&quot;:&quot;V-9&quot;,&quot;currency&quot;:&quot;USD&quot;,&quot;document_type&quot;:&quot;invoice&quot;,&quot;document_reference&quot;:&quot;INV-002&quot;,&quot;document_date&quot;:&quot;2026-10-02&quot;,&quot;outstanding_minor&quot;:20000},\n    {&quot;record_id&quot;:&quot;S3&quot;,&quot;entity_id&quot;:&quot;UK-1&quot;,&quot;supplier_id&quot;:&quot;V-9&quot;,&quot;currency&quot;:&quot;USD&quot;,&quot;document_type&quot;:&quot;credit_note&quot;,&quot;document_reference&quot;:&quot;CR-003&quot;,&quot;document_date&quot;:&quot;2026-10-03&quot;,&quot;outstanding_minor&quot;:5000}\n  ],\n  &quot;ledger_records&quot;: [\n    {&quot;record_id&quot;:&quot;L1&quot;,&quot;entity_id&quot;:&quot;UK-1&quot;,&quot;supplier_id&quot;:&quot;V-9&quot;,&quot;currency&quot;:&quot;USD&quot;,&quot;document_type&quot;:&quot;invoice&quot;,&quot;document_reference&quot;:&quot;INV-001&quot;,&quot;document_date&quot;:&quot;2026-10-01&quot;,&quot;outstanding_minor&quot;:12500},\n    {&quot;record_id&quot;:&quot;L2&quot;,&quot;entity_id&quot;:&quot;UK-1&quot;,&quot;supplier_id&quot;:&quot;V-9&quot;,&quot;currency&quot;:&quot;USD&quot;,&quot;document_type&quot;:&quot;invoice&quot;,&quot;document_reference&quot;:&quot;INV-002&quot;,&quot;document_date&quot;:&quot;2026-10-04&quot;,&quot;outstanding_minor&quot;:19000},\n    {&quot;record_id&quot;:&quot;L4&quot;,&quot;entity_id&quot;:&quot;UK-1&quot;,&quot;supplier_id&quot;:&quot;V-9&quot;,&quot;currency&quot;:&quot;USD&quot;,&quot;document_type&quot;:&quot;invoice&quot;,&quot;document_reference&quot;:&quot;INV-004&quot;,&quot;document_date&quot;:&quot;2026-10-05&quot;,&quot;outstanding_minor&quot;:7000}\n  ],\n  &quot;save&quot;: false\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The result has four groups: one match, one discrepancy with both <code>amount_mismatch</code>\nand <code>date_mismatch</code>, one item only in the statement, and one only in the ledger.\nEvery supplied row remains available. The credit note&#39;s amount is positive and is\nnot netted against invoices. No balance, loss or recoverable amount is calculated.\nIf you add another <code>INV-001</code> within either side&#39;s exact scope, its entire group\nbecomes <code>ambiguous_reference</code>; the tool does not choose the convenient amount pair.</p>\n<h2>Exact input contract and comparison rules</h2>\n<p>Supply a real <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code> <code>as_of</code> date and two arrays, each with 0\u20131000 records.\nAt least one side must contain a record. The date declares the intended common\nsnapshot; the tool cannot verify export freshness and does not filter rows by date.\nPrepare both lists with consistent open-item scope before comparing them.</p>\n<p>Each record requires <code>record_id</code>, <code>entity_id</code>, <code>supplier_id</code>, uppercase three-letter\n<code>currency</code>, <code>document_type</code> (<code>invoice</code> or <code>credit_note</code>), <code>document_reference</code>, real\nISO <code>document_date</code>, and positive safe-integer <code>outstanding_minor</code>. IDs/references\nare 1\u2013160 characters without surrounding whitespace or control characters. IDs\nmust be unique within a side; the two sides may use the same ID. Unknown fields,\nimpossible dates, year 0000, zero/negative/decimal/unsafe amounts and over-limit\narrays are rejected. Currency syntax is checked without an external currency list.</p>\n<p>Amounts are currency minor units: USD 12500 means $125.00. Credit notes use positive\nabsolute outstanding amounts and compare only with credit notes. Neither side is\nrequired to use a common supplier name, but its exact <code>supplier_id</code> must agree.\nDifferent entities, suppliers, currencies and document types remain separate.</p>\n<p>The join key is exact <code>(entity_id, supplier_id, currency, document_type, document_reference)</code>. Leading zeros, punctuation, case, accents, Unicode forms and\nlookalike characters remain significant. No supplier inference, reference cleanup,\ngreedy amount matching, payment allocation or currency conversion occurs.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Group status</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>matched</code></td>\n<td>Exactly one row on each side; key, outstanding amount and document date agree.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>only_in_statement</code></td>\n<td>One statement row has no exact-key ledger row.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>only_in_ledger</code></td>\n<td>One ledger row has no exact-key statement row.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>discrepancy</code></td>\n<td>Exactly one row on each side shares a key, but amount and/or date differs; <code>issues</code> preserves both reasons.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ambiguous_reference</code></td>\n<td>A key repeats on either side; all rows are retained without selecting pairs.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Missing rows and disagreements can result from export timing or scope. Repeated\nreferences can be legitimate. Even complete agreement between supplied lists is not\nproof that the supplier balance is correct: two incomplete exports can agree. The\nstatus <code>no_discrepancies_in_supplied_open_items</code> makes only that limited observation.\nSaved reports never become hidden cross-call history; include both lists every time.</p>\n<p>Groups and rows are deterministically sorted. Each source row belongs to exactly\none group; the engine does not expand every possible record pair. A group refers to\nsource IDs qualified by their statement/ledger side, so identical IDs across sides\ncannot overwrite evidence. No network or telemetry requests are made.</p>\n<h2>Free evidence, previews and CSV</h2>\n<p>Every check returns <code>retrieval.report_id</code>. Unsaved previews use <code>preview:&lt;UUID&gt;</code> and\nremain in memory for 30 minutes for the last two checks in this process. A third\ncheck, restart or expiry may evict a preview; resubmit the free check. Saved UUIDs\npersist on disk. Preview data is not written to a browser or filesystem.</p>\n<p><code>report_page</code> supports <code>statement_records</code>, <code>ledger_records</code>, <code>groups</code>, and\n<code>group_members</code>. Group pages contain summary metadata and side-specific member\ncounts. Read complete source members with <code>section:&quot;group_members&quot;</code> and the exact\n<code>group_id</code>. Follow <code>next_offset</code> until null. Pages and CSV remain free even after\nthe saved-report cap is reached.</p>\n<p>Responses use a 512 KiB escaped MCP text-envelope budget. Larger reports return a\npaginated overview; pages can contain fewer rows than requested to fit that budget.\nCSV uses one row per original source record with its side, original fields, group\nand reasons. Formula-like text is escaped; JSON source values are unchanged. Export\nreturns CSV text, never writes to a supplied filename, and repeats the header on\neach page. Retain one header when joining pages. Follow actual <code>returned</code> and\n<code>next_offset</code> values rather than assuming the requested count was returned.</p>\n<h2>Free and Pro</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Capability</th>\n<th>Free</th>\n<th>Pro</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Complete checks, discrepancy reasons and original source rows</td>\n<td>Included, 1000 records per side</td>\n<td>Same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Preview pages and CSV</td>\n<td>Included</td>\n<td>Same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Saved report count</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>No report-count cap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aggregate saved storage</td>\n<td>64 MiB</td>\n<td>64 MiB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Per-file integrity bound</td>\n<td>8 MiB</td>\n<td>8 MiB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Input, response, text and preview limits</td>\n<td>Same documented bounds</td>\n<td>Same</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>save:true</code> at the free cap returns the diagnostic and free preview with\n<code>saved:false</code>, <code>save_error:&quot;free_saved_report_cap&quot;</code>, and an upgrade link. Delete a\nsaved report to reuse a slot. Storage failures also preserve the diagnostic.</p>\n<p><a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/buy/supplier-statement-check?src=store.s.supplier-statement-check">Get Pro: $19 one time</a>.\n<a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/buy/bundle?src=store.s.supplier-statement-check">All servers: $39 bundle</a>. Set <code>MCP_LICENSE_KEY</code> or\ncall <code>license_activate</code>; keys verify offline. Pro removes only the saved-report\ncount cap. It adds no accounting data, accuracy guarantee, ERP access or safety\napproval. The saved-history upgrade is a commercial hypothesis, not proven ROI.</p>\n<p>All data stays local. Reports live under\n<code>${XDG_DATA_HOME:-~/.local/share}/mcp-servers/supplier-statement-check/</code>.\n<code>XDG_DATA_HOME</code> must be absolute with real directory ancestors. Private JSON files\nare written atomically with cross-process mutation locks. Reads validate original\ninputs and recompute evidence rather than trusting edited results. License\nactivation writes the shared local license configuration. Manage accounting-data\naccess, retention and backups yourself.</p>\n<h2>Development</h2>\n<pre><code class="language-sh">npm run build --workspace @theluckystrike/mcp-supplier-statement-check\nnpm test --workspace @theluckystrike/mcp-supplier-statement-check\n</code></pre>\n<p>Build Docker from the monorepo root with\n<code>docker build -f servers/supplier-statement-check/Dockerfile .</code>. The standalone\nmirror receives its own self-contained Dockerfile during mirror generation.\nTests cover the comparison oracle, strict inputs, scope/Unicode isolation, complete\nsource coverage, ambiguity, large free pages/CSV, safe storage, concurrent save\ncaps and actual stdio calls. Signed-license tests use the existing local private\nsigner when available and skip if it is absent. Private signing keys are not\npackaged or required by users.</p>\n\n<h2>First five minutes</h2>\n<p>Three prompts that scored 3 of 3, measured in round 47, 2026-10-26. Paste one in as it is written.</p>\n<p class="muted">Synthetic operator checks with frozen structured arguments through actual MCP stdio. These prompts describe the tasks; model translation, installation time and buyer outcomes were not tested.</p><pre class="prompt">Compare this supplier statement invoice with its AP open item. Preserve both original amounts and dates when the same exact reference disagrees.</pre>\n<p class="muted">Paste this into Claude with the server connected.</p>\n<p class="muted">What it did, measured in round 47, 2026-10-26: Actual stdio returned 1 exact-key groups, 0 matching and 1 requiring review, retaining all 2 original source items.</p>\n<pre class="prompt">Find which exact invoice references appear only on the supplier statement or only in the AP ledger, retaining every source item.</pre>\n<p class="muted">Paste this into Claude with the server connected.</p>\n<p class="muted">What it did, measured in round 47, 2026-10-26: Actual stdio returned 2 exact-key groups, 0 matching and 2 requiring review, retaining all 2 original source items.</p>\n<pre class="prompt">The supplier statement repeats the same invoice reference twice. Keep all three source items and do not guess which statement row matches the ledger.</pre>\n<p class="muted">Paste this into Claude with the server connected.</p>\n<p class="muted">What it did, measured in round 47, 2026-10-26: Actual stdio returned 1 exact-key groups, 0 matching and 1 requiring review, retaining all 3 original source items.</p>\n<p class="muted">On the free tier for this path: Full diagnostics for up to 1000 records per side, complete free evidence pages and CSV; store 3 reports at a time.</p>\n\n<p>Or <a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/buy/bundle?src=store.s.supplier-statement-check.bundle">the 49-server bundle for $39</a>.</p>', "first_five": [{ "round": 47, "date": "2026-10-26", "file": "user_value_r47.json", "id": "supplier1", "score": 3, "prompt": "Compare this supplier statement invoice with its AP open item. Preserve both original amounts and dates when the same exact reference disagrees.", "evidence": "Actual stdio returned 1 exact-key groups, 0 matching and 1 requiring review, retaining all 2 original source items." }, { "round": 47, "date": "2026-10-26", "file": "user_value_r47.json", "id": "supplier2", "score": 3, "prompt": "Find which exact invoice references appear only on the supplier statement or only in the AP ledger, retaining every source item.", "evidence": "Actual stdio returned 2 exact-key groups, 0 matching and 2 requiring review, retaining all 2 original source items." }, { "round": 47, "date": "2026-10-26", "file": "user_value_r47.json", "id": "supplier3", "score": 3, "prompt": "The supplier statement repeats the same invoice reference twice. Keep all three source items and do not guess which statement row matches the ledger.", "evidence": "Actual stdio returned 1 exact-key groups, 0 matching and 1 requiring review, retaining all 3 original source items." }], "first_five_round": 47, "first_five_date": "2026-10-26" }, "duplicate-invoice-check": { "title": "MCP Duplicate Invoice Check \u2014 Supplier Invoice History Review", "tagline": "Review duplicate supplier invoice candidates in supplied local history.", "description": "Compare structured invoice history within exact entity, supplier, currency and document-type boundaries. Show repeated references, conservative formatting collisions and same-amount/date review candidates with complete source evidence. Runs locally; no ERP or PDF parsing.", "html": '<h1>Duplicate supplier invoice check MCP server</h1>\n<p>Review a supplied batch of supplier invoices for repeated invoice numbers, conservative formatting variations, or identical amounts and invoice dates. This local MCP server returns complete grouped evidence and the exact reason each record needs review. Distinct legitimate invoices can share an amount and date. Candidates are not confirmed duplicates, fraud scores, recoverable money or payment decisions. It does not connect to an ERP, read PDFs, use OCR, or retrieve missing history.</p>\n<p>Built by <a href="https://github.com/theluckystrike">theluckystrike</a>. MIT. Support: <a href="mailto:support@zovo.one">support@zovo.one</a>.</p>\n<h2>Try it before installing</h2>\n<p><a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/guides/check-duplicate-supplier-invoice-references-in-claude#duplicate-demo">Run the free duplicate-invoice example in your browser</a>. Edit the synthetic invoice records to see candidate groups and their reasons. The example runs locally in your browser without an account or uploads. <a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/s/duplicate-invoice-check">Product details and installation options</a>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Your review task</th>\n<th>Start here</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Find repeated invoice references across a supplied invoice-history batch</td>\n<td>This server: <code>duplicate_invoice_check</code>. Candidates require review and are not confirmed duplicate payments.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compare one supplier invoice with its PO, accepted receipts and prior billed quantities</td>\n<td><a href="https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-three-way-match">Three-way supplier invoice matching</a>, with a <a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/guides/match-supplier-invoice-purchase-order-delivery-note-in-claude#matcher-demo">free browser example</a>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Both servers include their full diagnostics and CSV evidence for free. Pro removes the limit of three saved reports; the $39 bundle covers both. Neither tool approves payment or retrieves missing accounting history.</p>\n<h2>Install</h2>\n<p>Claude Desktop: download <a href="https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-servers/releases/download/v0.22.8/duplicate-invoice-check.mcpb">duplicate-invoice-check.mcpb v0.22.8</a> and open it. SHA-256: <code>181379eb6dcff3d90b55e080dd9c180f6c96802e991872865a6b0ce422b3aaea</code>. Other clients can use the source instructions below.</p>\n<p>Requires Node.js 18 or newer and an MCP client. No API key or account is needed for free use. The npm package is not published: build the source checkout, then use an absolute local path.</p>\n<pre><code class="language-sh">git clone https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-servers.git\ncd mcp-servers\nnpm install\nnode scripts/bootstrap-timezone.mjs\nnpm run build --workspace @theluckystrike/mcp-license\nnpm run build --workspace @theluckystrike/mcp-duplicate-invoice-check\n</code></pre>\n<p>Claude Desktop: merge this entry into <code>mcpServers</code> in <code>~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json</code> on macOS, or <code>%APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json</code> on Windows. Replace <code>/absolute/path</code> with the checkout location.</p>\n<pre><code class="language-json">{&quot;mcpServers&quot;:{&quot;duplicate-invoice-check&quot;:{&quot;command&quot;:&quot;node&quot;,&quot;args&quot;:[&quot;/absolute/path/mcp-servers/servers/duplicate-invoice-check/dist/index.js&quot;]}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Claude Code:</p>\n<pre><code class="language-sh">claude mcp add duplicate-invoice-check -- node /absolute/path/mcp-servers/servers/duplicate-invoice-check/dist/index.js\n</code></pre>\n<p>Cursor: put this local entry in <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code> in your project or <code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class="language-json">{&quot;mcpServers&quot;:{&quot;duplicate-invoice-check&quot;:{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;stdio&quot;,&quot;command&quot;:&quot;node&quot;,&quot;args&quot;:[&quot;/absolute/path/mcp-servers/servers/duplicate-invoice-check/dist/index.js&quot;]}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>VS Code: use <code>.vscode/mcp.json</code> with the top-level key <code>servers</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class="language-json">{&quot;servers&quot;:{&quot;duplicate-invoice-check&quot;:{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;stdio&quot;,&quot;command&quot;:&quot;node&quot;,&quot;args&quot;:[&quot;/absolute/path/mcp-servers/servers/duplicate-invoice-check/dist/index.js&quot;]}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Windsurf&#39;s legacy Cascade agent: merge the Claude Desktop <code>mcpServers</code> JSON into <code>~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json</code>. This file applies to Cascade; its newer Devin Local agent uses a different configuration route. The standalone server exposes eight tools.</p>\n<p>Cline: open its MCP Servers panel, Configure tab, then Configure MCP Servers. Add this entry to the settings JSON (for the Cline CLI, the file is <code>~/.cline/mcp.json</code>):</p>\n<pre><code class="language-json">{&quot;mcpServers&quot;:{&quot;duplicate-invoice-check&quot;:{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;stdio&quot;,&quot;command&quot;:&quot;node&quot;,&quot;args&quot;:[&quot;/absolute/path/mcp-servers/servers/duplicate-invoice-check/dist/index.js&quot;],&quot;disabled&quot;:false,&quot;autoApprove&quot;:[]}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>These are the six local stdio client configurations used by this project. There is no hosted endpoint or browser chat connector. If the client cannot find <code>node</code>, replace it with the absolute path printed by <code>which node</code>. After saving, reconnect the server (fully quit and restart Claude Desktop), call <code>license_status</code>, then run the example below. A <code>tools/list</code> result alone does not verify candidate checking.</p>\n<h2>Tools</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Input and result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>duplicate_invoice_check</code></td>\n<td><code>records</code>, optional <code>save</code> (false); complete grouped candidates, unique counts, source evidence and a free preview id.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>report_list</code></td>\n<td>Optional <code>offset</code> (0), <code>limit</code> (20, max 100); saved UUIDs and count summaries.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>report_get</code></td>\n<td>Saved <code>id</code>; source records, groups and reasons.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>report_page</code></td>\n<td>Saved or preview <code>id</code>, <code>section</code>, optional <code>offset</code>, <code>limit</code> (max 100), <code>group_id</code>; complete evidence pages.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>report_delete</code></td>\n<td>Saved <code>id</code>; delete one report and reuse its storage slot.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>report_export_csv</code></td>\n<td>Saved or preview <code>id</code>, optional <code>offset</code>, <code>limit</code> (max 1000); one row per source record, candidate reasons and group ids.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>license_status</code></td>\n<td>No arguments; offline tier and checkout link.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>license_activate</code></td>\n<td><code>key</code>; verify and store an offline Pro license.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>A repeated reference and a legitimate equal total</h2>\n<p>Call <code>duplicate_invoice_check</code> with synthetic records:</p>\n<pre><code class="language-json">{\n  &quot;records&quot;: [\n    {&quot;record_id&quot;:&quot;A&quot;,&quot;entity_id&quot;:&quot;UK-1&quot;,&quot;supplier_id&quot;:&quot;S-9&quot;,&quot;document_type&quot;:&quot;invoice&quot;,&quot;invoice_number&quot;:&quot;INV-001&quot;,&quot;invoice_date&quot;:&quot;2026-10-01&quot;,&quot;currency&quot;:&quot;USD&quot;,&quot;total_minor&quot;:12500},\n    {&quot;record_id&quot;:&quot;B&quot;,&quot;entity_id&quot;:&quot;UK-1&quot;,&quot;supplier_id&quot;:&quot;S-9&quot;,&quot;document_type&quot;:&quot;invoice&quot;,&quot;invoice_number&quot;:&quot;INV-001&quot;,&quot;invoice_date&quot;:&quot;2026-10-02&quot;,&quot;currency&quot;:&quot;USD&quot;,&quot;total_minor&quot;:13000},\n    {&quot;record_id&quot;:&quot;C&quot;,&quot;entity_id&quot;:&quot;UK-1&quot;,&quot;supplier_id&quot;:&quot;S-9&quot;,&quot;document_type&quot;:&quot;invoice&quot;,&quot;invoice_number&quot;:&quot;inv /001&quot;,&quot;invoice_date&quot;:&quot;2026-10-03&quot;,&quot;currency&quot;:&quot;USD&quot;,&quot;total_minor&quot;:12500},\n    {&quot;record_id&quot;:&quot;D&quot;,&quot;entity_id&quot;:&quot;UK-1&quot;,&quot;supplier_id&quot;:&quot;S-9&quot;,&quot;document_type&quot;:&quot;invoice&quot;,&quot;invoice_number&quot;:&quot;SERVICE-02&quot;,&quot;invoice_date&quot;:&quot;2026-10-01&quot;,&quot;currency&quot;:&quot;USD&quot;,&quot;total_minor&quot;:12500}\n  ],\n  &quot;save&quot;: false\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The result has four unique candidate records and three groups. <code>exact_reference</code> includes A/B despite their different dates and totals. <code>normalized_reference</code> includes A/B/C and exposes every conflicting field. <code>same_amount_date</code> includes A/D: two different legitimate services could have the same date and total, so this group is a review prompt. A appears in three groups but is counted once. No sum of possible losses is calculated. Changing C to <code>INV-1</code> excludes it from the normalized <code>INV001</code> group because leading zeros remain meaningful.</p>\n<h2>Exact input contract</h2>\n<p>Supply 1\u20131000 records, each with unique <code>record_id</code>, exact <code>entity_id</code> and <code>supplier_id</code>, <code>document_type</code> (<code>invoice</code> or <code>credit_note</code>), <code>invoice_number</code>, real <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code> <code>invoice_date</code>, uppercase three-letter <code>currency</code>, and positive safe-integer <code>total_minor</code>. IDs and invoice numbers are 1\u2013160 characters without surrounding whitespace or control characters. Optional <code>entity_label</code> and <code>supplier_label</code> are display-only text, up to 200 characters without controls. Unknown fields are rejected.</p>\n<p>Amounts are integer currency minor units, never floating point amounts: USD 12500 means $125.00. Currency codes are checked for uppercase three-letter syntax, not against an external currency registry. Credit notes use positive absolute amounts and compare only with credit notes. No amounts are added, converted or interpreted as a financial loss. Year 0000, impossible calendar dates, duplicate record ids, zero/negative/decimal/unsafe money and over-limit batches are input errors.</p>\n<p>Each rule groups only records with exactly equal <code>(entity_id, supplier_id, document_type, currency)</code>. Entity and supplier IDs are case-sensitive: similar names, different IDs and different entities never join. Multiple entities can appear in a batch, but each remains a separate scope. Display labels do not affect matching.</p>\n<p>Three equality rules run independently:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code>exact_reference</code>: the original invoice number matches exactly, even when amounts/dates differ.</li>\n<li><code>normalized_reference</code>: ASCII <code>a-z</code> becomes <code>A-Z</code>; only ASCII space, hyphen, slash and dot are removed. Leading zeros, accents, all Unicode characters, underscores, nonbreaking spaces, Unicode dashes and letter/digit lookalikes remain unchanged. Empty normalized keys do not collide.</li>\n<li><code>same_amount_date</code>: positive amount and invoice date both match exactly. This frequently has legitimate explanations.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Groups contain complete membership, exact scope and match key, an explanation and differing source fields. They are independent overlapping reasons, not transitive confirmed-duplicate clusters. <code>candidate_record_count</code> counts the union once. <code>no_candidates_in_supplied_batch</code> means these limited rules found none in the records supplied; it does not mean safe to pay. Missing history and reference, date or amount changes can evade these rules. Saved reports never become implicit cross-call history: include all records you want compared every time.</p>\n<p>Source records and report groups are sorted deterministically; reordering the same input records produces the same diagnostic evidence. The computation groups keys instead of producing every pair: at most 3000 memberships for 1000 records, including overlap. The server performs no telemetry or network requests.</p>\n<h2>Free evidence, paging and CSV</h2>\n<p>Every check returns a <code>retrieval.report_id</code>, including unsaved checks. Preview IDs are <code>preview:&lt;UUID&gt;</code>, local to the current server process. The last two previews remain available for 30 minutes. A third check, restart or expiry can evict a preview; resubmit the original free diagnostic. Saved UUIDs persist on disk.</p>\n<p><code>report_page</code> sections are <code>records</code>, <code>groups</code>, <code>record_summaries</code>, and <code>group_members</code>. Follow <code>next_offset</code> until null, with <code>limit</code> 1\u2013100. Group pages return summary metadata and <code>member_count</code>; read all members using <code>section: &quot;group_members&quot;</code> and the exact <code>group_id</code>. Member pages contain complete original records. Records are stored once in the report; groups refer to their exact record ids. All pages and CSV exports are free, including after hitting the saved-report cap.</p>\n<p>The encoded incoming stdio message must fit the SDK&#39;s 10 MiB receive buffer. Responses use an 8 MiB escaped text-envelope budget; a larger report returns an explicit paginated overview with the same free retrieval instructions. Current 1000-row/text limits fit that budget in transport tests, including worst-case quoting. CSV uses one row per record rather than repeating it for each group. It includes original source fields, candidate flag, reason codes and group ids. Formula-like leading text is escaped. Export returns text; it never writes to a caller-supplied filename. CSV pages can contain fewer rows than the requested limit when quoting would exceed the response budget. Follow the actual <code>next_offset</code> until null; <code>returned</code> always counts the rows in that page. Every CSV page repeats its header; retain one header when joining pages.</p>\n<h2>Free and Pro</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Capability</th>\n<th>Free</th>\n<th>Pro</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>All checks, source records, grouped reasons</td>\n<td>Included, up to 1000 records/call</td>\n<td>Same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Preview pages and CSV</td>\n<td>Included</td>\n<td>Same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concurrent saved reports</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>No report-count cap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total saved storage</td>\n<td>64 MiB</td>\n<td>64 MiB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Input/output, text and preview safety bounds</td>\n<td>Same documented bounds</td>\n<td>Same</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>save: true</code> at the free cap still returns the complete diagnostic and free preview with <code>saved: false</code>, <code>save_error: &quot;free_saved_report_cap&quot;</code>, and an upgrade link. Delete a saved report to reuse a slot. Storage failures also preserve diagnostics. Pro removes only the saved-count cap; it does not increase input, transport, preview or total storage bounds.</p>\n<p><a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/buy/duplicate-invoice-check?src=store.s.duplicate-invoice-check">Get Pro: $19 one time</a>. <a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/buy/bundle?src=store.s.duplicate-invoice-check">All servers: $39 bundle</a>. Set <code>MCP_LICENSE_KEY</code> or call <code>license_activate</code>. Keys verify offline.</p>\n<p>All data stays local. Reports live under <code>${XDG_DATA_HOME:-~/.local/share}/mcp-servers/duplicate-invoice-check/</code>. <code>XDG_DATA_HOME</code> must be absolute and directory ancestry must contain no symlinks. Private JSON files use atomic writes and cross-process mutation locks; reads revalidate source records and recompute the evidence instead of trusting edited derived results. License activation writes the shared local license config. Manage local accounting-data access and backups yourself.</p>\n<h2>Development</h2>\n<pre><code class="language-sh">npm run build --workspace @theluckystrike/mcp-duplicate-invoice-check\nnpm test --workspace @theluckystrike/mcp-duplicate-invoice-check\n</code></pre>\n<p>Build Docker from the repository root: <code>docker build -f servers/duplicate-invoice-check/Dockerfile .</code>. Tests cover actual stdio calls, schema rejection, scope/Unicode/zero negatives, an independent 2000-batch pairwise oracle, grouped 1000-record transport, pages and CSV, cross-process save caps, recomputation, symlink/path rejection and aggregate storage boundaries. A local signed-key stdio test uses the operator&#39;s existing signer if present; it skips when that private signer is absent.</p>\n\n<h2>First five minutes</h2>\n<p>Three prompts that scored 3 of 3, measured in round 46, 2026-10-16. Paste one in as it is written.</p>\n<pre class="prompt">The same supplier sent INV-001 twice with different dates and totals. Preserve both values and show why review is needed.</pre>\n<p class="muted">Paste this into Claude with the server connected.</p>\n<p class="muted">What it did, measured in round 46, 2026-10-16: Two distinct candidate records in exact and normalized groups; Original dates and amounts preserved as explicit conflicting fields</p>\n<pre class="prompt">Find INV-001 versus inv /001 but keep INV-1 separate: leading zeros matter.</pre>\n<p class="muted">Paste this into Claude with the server connected.</p>\n<p class="muted">What it did, measured in round 46, 2026-10-16: Formatting change produces one two-record review group; Leading-zero difference excluded; All three original records retained</p>\n<pre class="prompt">These identical references belong to another entity, supplier, currency or a credit note. Do not merge them.</pre>\n<p class="muted">Paste this into Claude with the server connected.</p>\n<p class="muted">What it did, measured in round 46, 2026-10-16: Five source records retained; Zero candidate groups across distinct exact scopes; Zero means no candidates in supplied batch, not payment clearance</p>\n<p class="muted">On the free tier for this path: Full diagnostics for up to 1000 supplied records, complete free paging and CSV export; store 3 reports at a time.</p>\n\n<p>Or <a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/buy/bundle?src=store.s.duplicate-invoice-check.bundle">the 49-server bundle for $39</a>.</p>', "first_five": [{ "round": 46, "date": "2026-10-16", "file": "user_value_r46.json", "id": "dup1", "score": 3, "prompt": "The same supplier sent INV-001 twice with different dates and totals. Preserve both values and show why review is needed.", "evidence": "Two distinct candidate records in exact and normalized groups; Original dates and amounts preserved as explicit conflicting fields" }, { "round": 46, "date": "2026-10-16", "file": "user_value_r46.json", "id": "dup2", "score": 3, "prompt": "Find INV-001 versus inv /001 but keep INV-1 separate: leading zeros matter.", "evidence": "Formatting change produces one two-record review group; Leading-zero difference excluded; All three original records retained" }, { "round": 46, "date": "2026-10-16", "file": "user_value_r46.json", "id": "dup3", "score": 3, "prompt": "These identical references belong to another entity, supplier, currency or a credit note. Do not merge them.", "evidence": "Five source records retained; Zero candidate groups across distinct exact scopes; Zero means no candidates in supplied batch, not payment clearance" }], "first_five_round": 46, "first_five_date": "2026-10-16" }, "three-way-match": { "title": "MCP Three-Way Invoice Match \u2014 Supplier Invoice, PO and Receipt Checks", "tagline": "Supplier invoice, purchase order and receipt checks by exact SKU.", "description": "Compare structured supplier invoice lines with a purchase order and accepted receipt quantities. Flag price, quantity, reference and currency exceptions with source evidence. Runs locally; no ERP connection or document parsing.", "html": '<h1>Three-way supplier invoice matching MCP server</h1>\n<p>Check a supplier invoice against its purchase order and delivery receipts in Claude or another MCP client. This local server compares each exact SKU, unit price and billed quantity with the PO, cumulative accepted deliveries and quantities you say were already invoiced. It catches offsetting SKU mistakes even when the invoice and PO grand totals agree, and returns the source values behind every exception. Supply structured documents; this server does not extract PDFs, perform OCR, connect to an ERP, post payments or approve an invoice.</p>\n<p>Built by <a href="https://github.com/theluckystrike">theluckystrike</a>. MIT. Support: <a href="mailto:support@zovo.one">support@zovo.one</a>.</p>\n<h2>Try it before installing</h2>\n<p><a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/guides/match-supplier-invoice-purchase-order-delivery-note-in-claude#matcher-demo">Run the free three-way matching example in your browser</a>. Edit the synthetic PO, receipt and invoice fields to see per-SKU exceptions. The example runs locally in your browser without an account or uploads. <a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/s/three-way-match">Product details and installation options</a>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Your review task</th>\n<th>Start here</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Compare one supplier invoice with its PO, accepted receipts and prior billed quantities</td>\n<td>This server: <code>three_way_match</code>. Equal grand totals can still hide SKU errors.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Find repeated invoice references across a supplied invoice-history batch</td>\n<td><a href="https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-duplicate-invoice-check">Duplicate supplier invoice check</a>, with a <a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/guides/check-duplicate-supplier-invoice-references-in-claude#duplicate-demo">free browser example</a>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Both servers include their full diagnostics and CSV evidence for free. Pro removes the limit of three saved reports; the $39 bundle covers both. Neither tool approves payment or retrieves missing accounting history.</p>\n<h2>Install</h2>\n<p>Claude Desktop: download <a href="https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-servers/releases/download/v0.22.8/three-way-match.mcpb">three-way-match.mcpb v0.22.8</a> and open it. SHA-256: <code>6397928ec44ea9d5c406a66f4eb7aa9a2f34f5731bf8ef9972fadfdac882f040</code>. Other clients can use the source instructions below.</p>\n<p>Requires Node.js 18 or newer. Build from the source checkout; the npm package is not published. No API key or account is required to run the free tools.</p>\n<pre><code class="language-sh">git clone https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-servers.git\ncd mcp-servers\nnpm install\nnode scripts/bootstrap-timezone.mjs\nnpm run build --workspace @theluckystrike/mcp-license\nnpm run build --workspace @theluckystrike/mcp-three-way-match\n</code></pre>\n<p>Use the absolute path to <code>servers/three-way-match/dist/index.js</code> in your checkout. The server speaks stdio; your MCP client starts it. Do not put an HTTP URL in its place.</p>\n<p>Claude Desktop: merge this entry into <code>mcpServers</code> in <code>~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json</code> on macOS (or <code>%APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json</code> on Windows), replacing <code>/absolute/path</code> with your checkout location:</p>\n<pre><code class="language-json">{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;three-way-match&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;node&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [&quot;/absolute/path/mcp-servers/servers/three-way-match/dist/index.js&quot;]\n    }\n  }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Claude Code:</p>\n<pre><code class="language-sh">claude mcp add three-way-match -- node /absolute/path/mcp-servers/servers/three-way-match/dist/index.js\n</code></pre>\n<p>Cursor: merge the same JSON into <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code> in your project or <code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code>. Restart your client, call <code>license_status</code>, then run the example below. A <code>tools/list</code> result alone does not verify matching.</p>\n<p>For Claude Desktop, download <a href="https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-servers/releases/download/v0.22.8/three-way-match.mcpb">three-way-match.mcpb</a> and open it to install. SHA-256: <code>6397928ec44ea9d5c406a66f4eb7aa9a2f34f5731bf8ef9972fadfdac882f040</code>. This is a local stdio server; no hosted endpoint is available.</p>\n<h2>Tools</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Arguments and result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>three_way_match</code></td>\n<td>PO, receipts, invoice, required <code>prior_invoiced</code>, optional <code>save</code>; returns full report and save outcome.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>report_list</code></td>\n<td>Optional <code>offset</code> (0), <code>limit</code> (20, maximum 100); returns saved UUIDs and summaries.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>report_get</code></td>\n<td><code>id</code>; source documents, all issues and per-SKU evidence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>report_page</code></td>\n<td><code>id</code>, <code>section</code>, optional <code>offset</code>/<code>limit</code>, <code>receipt_index</code>/<code>sku</code>; free pages of every issue, SKU evidence and source row.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>report_delete</code></td>\n<td><code>id</code>; removes that saved report and frees a storage slot.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>report_export_csv</code></td>\n<td><code>id</code>, optional <code>offset</code>/<code>limit</code>; CSV text plus currency, status and document-level issue codes; large exports paginate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>license_status</code></td>\n<td>No arguments; offline free/Pro status and checkout link.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>license_activate</code></td>\n<td><code>key</code>; verifies and stores a Pro key offline.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>Input contract</h2>\n<p>One PO and one current supplier invoice per call. There is no fuzzy matching: SKU, supplier and PO references are exact, case-sensitive strings, with no surrounding whitespace or control characters. Document references and SKUs are 1\u2013120 characters. PO, invoice and receipt document references must be distinct. Duplicate SKUs within a document or within <code>prior_invoiced</code>, and duplicate receipt references, produce <code>invalid</code>; ambiguous rows are excluded from comparison. Repeated SKUs across different receipt documents are expected and accumulate.</p>\n<p>Quantities are whole units from 0 to 1,000,000,000; PO and invoice line quantities must be positive. Fractional quantities are rejected. <code>accepted_quantity</code> and <code>rejected_quantity</code> are disjoint counts, not a gross count and its subset. Rejected units never increase the quantity available to invoice. Receipts must carry valid <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code> dates; include only receipts relevant to this check. Dates are evidence, not an automatic invoice-date cutoff.</p>\n<p>Every monetary input is an integer minor-unit amount from 0 to <code>Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER</code> (9,007,199,254,740,991). For USD, 100 means $1.00; for a zero-decimal currency use its own minor units. Supply the uppercase three-letter currency code on both PO and invoice. No currency conversion or currency-code catalog lookup occurs. Unit prices must match exactly, with no tolerance. Money products and totals use integer arithmetic; unsafe results produce <code>invalid</code> with <code>unsafe_money_arithmetic</code>, never a rounded amount.</p>\n<p><code>invoice.total_minor</code> is the total of the supplied goods lines. There are no implicit tax, freight, discount, rounding or credit adjustments. Represent agreed charges as explicit priced PO/invoice SKUs and appropriate receipt evidence, or prepare an excluding-adjustments goods total before calling. Do not supply an all-in invoice total while omitting its adjustments.</p>\n<p><code>prior_invoiced</code> is required, even when empty. Supply the complete cumulative quantities previously billed for each SKU against this PO, excluding this current invoice. <code>[]</code> explicitly asserts none. Current plus prior quantities must fit both accepted receipts and the PO. Saving reports does not update this history or detect repeated invoice references across checks; incorrect or omitted history can allow repeat billing to go undetected. Reports are evidence snapshots, not an accounting ledger.</p>\n<p>Bounds apply equally to free and Pro: 1\u2013500 PO lines, 1\u2013500 invoice lines, up to 500 prior SKUs, and up to 100 receipts of 1\u2013500 lines each. An empty receipts array is valid and produces missing-receipt exceptions for billed PO SKUs. Empty or malformed references, blank/missing SKUs, decimals, unsafe input numbers and invalid dates are rejected as input errors. Unknown SKUs are explicit <code>unordered_sku</code> exceptions.</p>\n<h2>Equal totals can hide SKU errors</h2>\n<p>Call <code>three_way_match</code> with:</p>\n<pre><code class="language-json">{\n  &quot;po&quot;: {\n    &quot;reference&quot;: &quot;PO-1&quot;, &quot;supplier&quot;: &quot;Supply Co&quot;, &quot;currency&quot;: &quot;USD&quot;,\n    &quot;lines&quot;: [\n      {&quot;sku&quot;: &quot;A&quot;, &quot;quantity&quot;: 10, &quot;unit_price_minor&quot;: 100},\n      {&quot;sku&quot;: &quot;B&quot;, &quot;quantity&quot;: 10, &quot;unit_price_minor&quot;: 100}\n    ]\n  },\n  &quot;receipts&quot;: [{\n    &quot;reference&quot;: &quot;GR-1&quot;, &quot;po_reference&quot;: &quot;PO-1&quot;, &quot;supplier&quot;: &quot;Supply Co&quot;, &quot;date&quot;: &quot;2026-10-16&quot;,\n    &quot;lines&quot;: [\n      {&quot;sku&quot;: &quot;A&quot;, &quot;accepted_quantity&quot;: 5, &quot;rejected_quantity&quot;: 0},\n      {&quot;sku&quot;: &quot;B&quot;, &quot;accepted_quantity&quot;: 15, &quot;rejected_quantity&quot;: 0}\n    ]\n  }],\n  &quot;invoice&quot;: {\n    &quot;reference&quot;: &quot;INV-1&quot;, &quot;po_reference&quot;: &quot;PO-1&quot;, &quot;supplier&quot;: &quot;Supply Co&quot;, &quot;currency&quot;: &quot;USD&quot;,\n    &quot;lines&quot;: [\n      {&quot;sku&quot;: &quot;A&quot;, &quot;quantity&quot;: 10, &quot;unit_price_minor&quot;: 100},\n      {&quot;sku&quot;: &quot;B&quot;, &quot;quantity&quot;: 10, &quot;unit_price_minor&quot;: 100}\n    ],\n    &quot;total_minor&quot;: 2000\n  },\n  &quot;prior_invoiced&quot;: [],\n  &quot;save&quot;: false\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The report has <code>status: &quot;exceptions&quot;</code>, despite equal 2,000-minor-unit PO/invoice totals and equal aggregate quantities. SKU A has <code>invoiced_above_accepted</code> (10 billed, 5 accepted); SKU B has <code>accepted_above_order</code> (15 accepted, 10 ordered). Changing invoice unit prices to 90 for A and 110 for B also preserves the grand total but produces two <code>unit_price_mismatch</code> issues.</p>\n<h2>Partial deliveries can match</h2>\n<p>Using the same PO, supply a receipt containing only A with <code>accepted_quantity: 4</code>, <code>rejected_quantity: 2</code>. Invoice only A with <code>quantity: 4</code>, <code>unit_price_minor: 100</code>, <code>total_minor: 400</code>, and keep <code>prior_invoiced: []</code>. This returns <code>matched</code>. A has six units still undelivered; B need not appear on this partial invoice. A second matching receipt adds its accepted quantity; rejected quantities remain separate evidence. After previously billing two A units, supply <code>prior_invoiced: [{&quot;sku&quot;:&quot;A&quot;,&quot;quantity&quot;:2}]</code> and bill at most two more against those four accepted units.</p>\n<h2>Reading the report</h2>\n<p><code>matched</code> means the supplied evidence passes these exact checks; it does not authorize payment. <code>exceptions</code> identifies document mismatches, unknown SKUs, missing receipts, excessive accepted/billed quantities, price differences or an inconsistent declared invoice total. <code>invalid</code> identifies ambiguity or unsafe computed money; resolve it before interpreting the comparison. Mismatched receipt PO/supplier references are flagged and those receipts do not support billing.</p>\n<p>Every issue has <code>code</code>, <code>severity</code>, <code>source</code>, optional <code>sku</code>, and a concrete message. Each sorted SKU row includes PO/invoice one-based source line numbers; ordered, accepted, rejected, prior and current invoice quantities; both unit prices; cumulative billed quantity; available quantity before this invoice; remaining undelivered quantity; receipt references/line numbers and inclusion flags; and issue codes. The report retains the complete supplied documents, so a duplicate row never disappears from the audit evidence. Partial shortages are shown as remaining quantities and do not themselves fail a supported partial invoice.</p>\n<p>Small reports return inline. A report whose escaped MCP response would exceed 8 MiB returns an explicit <code>paginated: true</code> overview with status, totals, counts, source document headers and <code>report_id</code>. All evidence remains free through <code>report_page</code>. Select <code>section</code> from <code>issues</code>, <code>lines</code>, <code>po_lines</code>, <code>invoice_lines</code>, <code>prior_invoiced</code>, <code>receipts</code>, <code>receipt_lines</code> or <code>line_receipts</code>; use <code>offset</code> (default 0) and <code>limit</code> (default 100, maximum 100), following <code>next_offset</code> until null. <code>lines</code> pages contain scalar evidence and <code>receipt_count</code>; get those receipt entries with <code>line_receipts</code> and the exact <code>sku</code>. <code>receipt_lines</code> requires the zero-based <code>receipt_index</code> from the original receipt array. <code>receipts</code> pages contain document headers and line counts. Together these sections preserve every source row and every computed issue.</p>\n<p>Saved reports use their persistent UUID for pages, and <code>report_get</code> returns the same bounded overview for large saved reports. Large unsaved reports, including save-cap and storage failures, use <code>preview:&lt;UUID&gt;</code> IDs: the process keeps its last two large previews for 30 minutes. Restart, expiry or a third preview can evict them; resubmit the original diagnostic to recreate the free preview. Preview IDs belong to the current running server process. The underlying SDK also limits incoming stdio messages to 10 MiB; encoded requests must fit that transport limit even when individual row counts fit the schema.</p>\n<p>CSV includes one row per SKU, exact minor-unit prices, quantities and line issue codes; document issues remain in the accompanying JSON and full report. CSV is returned as text, not written to an arbitrary caller-supplied path. Formula-like leading characters are escaped for spreadsheet use.</p>\n<p>CSV exports that fit the same 8 MiB response budget stay inline. Larger exports return a first chunk of up to 1,000 rows with <code>paginated</code>, <code>offset</code>, <code>returned</code>, <code>total_rows</code> and <code>next_offset</code>. Pass that <code>next_offset</code> as <code>offset</code> in another <code>report_export_csv</code> call; optional <code>limit</code> is 1\u20131,000. Every chunk repeats the header; retain one header when combining chunks.</p>\n<h2>Free and Pro</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Capability</th>\n<th>Free</th>\n<th>Pro</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Full diagnostic checks and source evidence</td>\n<td>All checks, up to 500 PO/invoice lines</td>\n<td>Same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Receipts and prior invoice checks</td>\n<td>Same documented bounds</td>\n<td>Same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Saved reports</td>\n<td>3 at a time</td>\n<td>No report-count cap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>List/get/delete and CSV export</td>\n<td>Included</td>\n<td>Included</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>When <code>save: true</code> hits the free cap, the full report is still returned with <code>saved: false</code>, <code>save_error: &quot;free_saved_report_cap&quot;</code> and the checkout link. A storage error likewise preserves the diagnostic report. Delete an old saved report to reuse a free slot. A saved report file is limited to 64 MiB when saved and read; this is a storage integrity guard, not a Pro limit.</p>\n<p><a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/buy/three-way-match?src=store.s.three-way-match">Get Pro: $19 one time</a>. <a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/buy/bundle?src=store.s.three-way-match">All servers: $39 bundle</a>. Activate the key with <code>license_activate</code> or set <code>MCP_LICENSE_KEY</code>. Keys verify offline.</p>\n<p>All data stays local. Matching makes no network requests. Saved JSON reports live under <code>${XDG_DATA_HOME:-~/.local/share}/mcp-servers/three-way-match/</code>; <code>XDG_DATA_HOME</code> must be absolute and the storage directory ancestry must not contain symlinks. Files use atomic rename and mutations share a cross-process lock. Reports include commercial document data: manage local access and backups accordingly. License activation writes the shared local license config.</p>\n<h2>Development</h2>\n<pre><code class="language-sh">npm run build --workspace @theluckystrike/mcp-three-way-match\nnpm test --workspace @theluckystrike/mcp-three-way-match\n</code></pre>\n<p>Tests exercise offsetting quantity/price errors, rejected and multiple receipts, partials, explicit prior billing, unknown and duplicate SKUs/references, currency/PO/supplier mismatches, dates, integer boundaries, free/Pro save policy, atomic storage and an MCP stdio initialization/tools/call round trip. Docker builds from the repository root with <code>docker build -f servers/three-way-match/Dockerfile .</code>.</p>\n\n<h2>First five minutes</h2>\n<p>Three prompts that scored 3 of 3, measured in round 45, 2026-10-16. Paste one in as it is written.</p>\n<pre class="prompt">Check PO A10/B10 at100 each against accepted A5/B15 and invoice A10/B10 total2000. Prior invoiced is empty. Find mismatches hidden by totals.</pre>\n<p class="muted">Paste this into Claude with the server connected.</p>\n<p class="muted">What it did, measured in round 45, 2026-10-16: Equal 2000-minor-unit PO and invoice totals still produced two per-SKU exceptions: A has 10 billed against 5 accepted, and B has 15 accepted against 10 ordered.</p>\n<pre class="prompt">The order is A10 at100. Five arrived and five are billed for500. There were no previous invoices. Is this partial delivery supported?</pre>\n<p class="muted">Paste this into Claude with the server connected.</p>\n<p class="muted">What it did, measured in round 45, 2026-10-16: A partial delivery matched: 5 currently invoiced, 5 accepted, 10 ordered and zero previously invoiced at the same 100-minor-unit price.</p>\n<pre class="prompt">PO A10/B10 at100 each was fully delivered. Invoice A10 at110 and B10 at90 still totals2000. Flag both price changes and save the report.</pre>\n<p class="muted">Paste this into Claude with the server connected.</p>\n<p class="muted">What it did, measured in round 45, 2026-10-16: The unchanged 2000-minor-unit invoice total did not hide the two unit-price mismatches: A110 vs PO100, B90 vs PO100.</p>\n<p class="muted">On the free tier for this path: Full previews up to 500 PO/invoice lines, including free pages of all large-report evidence; store 3 reports at a time. Listing, retrieval, deletion and CSV export stay free.</p>\n\n<p>Or <a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/buy/bundle?src=store.s.three-way-match.bundle">the 49-server bundle for $39</a>.</p>', "first_five": [{ "round": 45, "date": "2026-10-16", "file": "user_value_r45.json", "id": "twm1", "score": 3, "prompt": "Check PO A10/B10 at100 each against accepted A5/B15 and invoice A10/B10 total2000. Prior invoiced is empty. Find mismatches hidden by totals.", "evidence": "Equal 2000-minor-unit PO and invoice totals still produced two per-SKU exceptions: A has 10 billed against 5 accepted, and B has 15 accepted against 10 ordered." }, { "round": 45, "date": "2026-10-16", "file": "user_value_r45.json", "id": "twm2", "score": 3, "prompt": "The order is A10 at100. Five arrived and five are billed for500. There were no previous invoices. Is this partial delivery supported?", "evidence": "A partial delivery matched: 5 currently invoiced, 5 accepted, 10 ordered and zero previously invoiced at the same 100-minor-unit price." }, { "round": 45, "date": "2026-10-16", "file": "user_value_r45.json", "id": "twm3", "score": 3, "prompt": "PO A10/B10 at100 each was fully delivered. Invoice A10 at110 and B10 at90 still totals2000. Flag both price changes and save the report.", "evidence": "The unchanged 2000-minor-unit invoice total did not hide the two unit-price mismatches: A110 vs PO100, B90 vs PO100." }], "first_five_round": 45, "first_five_date": "2026-10-16" }, "time-tracker": { "title": "MCP Time Tracking Server \u2014 Free, No Install", "tagline": "Track billable time without leaving the chat.", "description": "Track billable time from Claude with a free, no-install MCP server. Start and stop timers in chat, weekly reports and client billing.", "html": '<h1>Track time from Claude with a free, no-install server</h1>\n<p><strong>Featured on <a href="mcpservers.org">Awesome MCP Servers</a></strong> - <a href="https://mcpservers.org/servers/github-com-theluckystrike-mcp-servers-tree-main-servers-time-tracker">directory listing</a> | <a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/s/time-tracker">live hosted endpoint</a>, free tier, no signup.</p>\n<p>Track billable time without leaving your AI chat. Say &quot;start a timer on the acme redesign&quot;, keep working, then\nask for &quot;my hours this week by project&quot; or &quot;invoice lines for acme in August&quot;. It keeps a running timer, lets you\nlog time you forgot to track, applies your hourly rate per project, and turns the result into a report, a CSV file\nor a set of invoice line items. Everything is stored as plain JSON on your own machine.</p>\n<p>Built by <a href="https://github.com/theluckystrike">theluckystrike</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>In the <a href="https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io/v0.1/servers/io.github.theluckystrike%2Ftime-tracker-timesheet-billable-hours/versions/latest">official MCP Registry</a></strong> (<code>io.github.theluckystrike/time-tracker-timesheet-billable-hours</code>).</p>\n<p><strong>Listed on the <a href="https://index.percall.dev/l/zovo-time-tracker.html">AI Product Index</a></strong> - live remote endpoint at <a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/s/time-tracker">mcp.zovo.one/s/time-tracker</a>, free tier, no signup.</p>\n<p><img src="../../assets/demo-time-tracker.gif" alt="time-tracker demo"></p>\n<p>Track billable time from chat and turn it straight into a report or invoice line items, zero setup, all local.</p>\n<h2>60-second install</h2>\n<p>npm publish for <code>@theluckystrike/mcp-time-tracker</code> is pending. Until then, the <code>.mcpb</code> one-click bundle or a clone+build\nis the working path, both are verified below.</p>\n<p>One-click (.mcpb): download <code>time-tracker.mcpb</code> from the latest release and double-click it in Claude Desktop:\n<a href="https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-servers/releases/latest">https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-servers/releases/latest</a></p>\n<p>(<code>claude_desktop_config.json</code>):</p>\n<pre><code class="language-json">{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;time-tracker&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [&quot;-y&quot;, &quot;@theluckystrike/mcp-time-tracker&quot;]\n    }\n  }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Claude Code:</p>\n<pre><code class="language-sh">claude mcp add time-tracker -- npx -y @theluckystrike/mcp-time-tracker\n</code></pre>\n<p>(<code>.cursor/mcp.json</code>):</p>\n<pre><code class="language-json">{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;time-tracker&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [&quot;-y&quot;, &quot;@theluckystrike/mcp-time-tracker&quot;]\n    }\n  }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The <code>npx</code> form above starts working the moment the package is published. Until then, use the .mcpb bundle above, or\nbuild from source with exactly these three commands:</p>\n<pre><code class="language-sh">git clone https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-servers.git &amp;&amp; cd mcp-servers\nnpm install\nnpm run build -w packages/mcp-license -w servers/time-tracker\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then point your client&#39;s <code>command</code> at <code>node</code> with one arg: the absolute path to <code>servers/time-tracker/dist/index.js</code>.</p>\n<p>To run in Pro mode set <code>MCP_LICENSE_KEY</code> in the same config block, or call <code>license_activate</code> once with your key.</p>\n<h2>Tools</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>timer_start</code></td>\n<td>Start a timer on a project (optional task, tags, rate, currency). Starting a new one stops and logs the old one. A partial project name that matches exactly one existing project is used as that project.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>timer_stop</code></td>\n<td>Stop the running timer, write the entry, return the duration.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>timer_status</code></td>\n<td>What is running, for how long, and today&#39;s total.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>entry_add</code></td>\n<td>Log time you already worked (start plus end or minutes), with an optional rate and currency: rate &quot;90 euros an hour&quot; bills as EUR 225.00 for 2.5 h. Partial project names resolve like <code>timer_start</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>entry_list</code></td>\n<td>Compact table of entries, filtered by date range and project.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>entry_edit</code></td>\n<td>Change any field of an entry.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>entry_delete</code></td>\n<td>Delete an entry by id.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>project_set_rate</code></td>\n<td>Set the hourly rate and currency used for money totals (currency accepts codes or words: EUR, euros, pounds, zl). <code>apply_to_existing: true</code> re-rates time already logged for that project; add <code>only_missing: true</code> to touch only entries that carry no rate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>report</code></td>\n<td>Hours and money for a period, optionally grouped by project, day, task or tag; omit <code>group_by</code> for the plain total per currency. Table, JSON or CSV. Hours already invoiced are left out; pass <code>unbilled_only: false</code> for the full timesheet.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>export_csv</code></td>\n<td>Write entries to a CSV file and return the path.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>invoice_summary</code></td>\n<td>Invoice-ready line items for one project: hours, rate, amount, total, in the currency the time was logged in. One line per task and rate, so no line ever shows a blended rate nobody agreed. Returns the <code>entry_ids</code> behind the lines, and skips hours already invoiced (<code>unbilled_only: false</code> includes them). Free for the last 7 days, Pro for any period from full history.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>entry_mark_billed</code></td>\n<td>Stamp the hours that went on an invoice with its number (<code>ids</code> from <code>invoice_summary</code>, or <code>project</code> + <code>from</code> + <code>to</code>), so <code>report</code> and <code>invoice_summary</code> stop offering them and the same hours are never billed twice.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>license_status</code></td>\n<td>Free or Pro, and where to upgrade.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>license_activate</code></td>\n<td>Activate a Pro key (verified offline).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Also exposed: the resource <code>timetracker://today</code> (today&#39;s summary) and the prompt <code>daily_standup</code>\n(writes a standup update from yesterday&#39;s and today&#39;s tracked time).</p>\n<h2>What you can say</h2>\n<p>No tool names required. These are the sentences that were actually tested against the server; the tool\ncolumn is what answered them.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>You say</th>\n<th>Tool</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>&quot;Start a timer for the Acme website project.&quot;</td>\n<td><code>timer_start</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Stop the timer and tell me how long I worked.&quot;</td>\n<td><code>timer_stop</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What&#39;s running right now, and for how long?&quot;</td>\n<td><code>timer_status</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Log 2.5 hours yesterday for Acme, design review, at 90 euros an hour.&quot;</td>\n<td><code>entry_add</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Show me my Acme entries for this week.&quot;</td>\n<td><code>entry_list</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;That last entry should say API refactor, not design review.&quot;</td>\n<td><code>entry_edit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Delete the entry I just logged twice.&quot;</td>\n<td><code>entry_delete</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Set my rate for Acme to 90 EUR an hour.&quot;</td>\n<td><code>project_set_rate</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How many hours did I bill this month, grouped by project?&quot;</td>\n<td><code>report</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Give me invoice lines for Acme in August.&quot;</td>\n<td><code>invoice_summary</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;I invoiced those hours as INV-2026-0001.&quot;</td>\n<td><code>entry_mark_billed</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Two more worth knowing: &quot;export my time to a CSV for my bookkeeper&quot; (<code>export_csv</code>) and &quot;write my standup\nupdate from yesterday and today&quot; (the <code>daily_standup</code> prompt).</p>\n<h2>Worked example</h2>\n<p>This is a real transcript from the audit in <code>docs/USER_VALUE_R2.md</code>, numbers unchanged.</p>\n<pre><code>You: Log 2.5 hours yesterday for Acme, design review, at 90 euros an hour.\n\n  entry_add { project: &quot;Acme&quot;, ... }\n  -&gt; &quot;Acme&quot; resolved to the existing project &quot;Acme website&quot;\n  -&gt; rate &quot;90 euros an hour&quot; parsed as EUR 90.00/h\n\nAssistant: Logged 2.50 h for Acme website - design review at EUR 90.00/h = EUR 225.00.\n\nYou: How much do I bill Acme this week? Give me invoice lines.\n\n  invoice_summary { project: &quot;Acme website&quot;, from: &quot;2026-08-31&quot;, to: &quot;2026-09-02&quot; }\n\nAssistant: Invoice summary - Acme website\n  design review    2.50 h   EUR 90.00/h   EUR 225.00\n  TOTAL            2.50 h                 EUR 225.00\n</code></pre>\n<p>One call each. The rate carries its currency all the way through: the report never prints a bare &quot;225&quot;,\nand it never turns into &quot;$225&quot; by accident.</p>\n<p>A second worked example, the weekly report and the <code>daily_standup</code> prompt:</p>\n<pre><code>You: How many hours did I put into Acme this week, grouped by task?\n\n  report { project: &quot;Acme website&quot;, from: &quot;2026-08-27&quot;, to: &quot;2026-09-02&quot;, group_by: &quot;task&quot; }\n\nAssistant: Acme website, 2026-08-27 to 2026-09-02\n  design review   2.50 h   EUR 225.00\n  TOTAL           2.50 h   EUR 225.00\n\nYou: Write my standup update.\n\n  (prompt) daily_standup\n\nAssistant: Yesterday: 2.50 h on Acme website (design review).\nToday: nothing logged yet.\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Billed hours close</h3>\n<p>An hour that has been invoiced is finished. <code>entry_mark_billed {ids, invoice_number}</code> writes\n<code>billed_at</code> and <code>billed_invoice</code> onto those entries; from then on <code>report</code> and <code>invoice_summary</code>\nskip them by default, so next month&#39;s &quot;invoice Acme&quot; cannot re-bill work already paid for. The\nwhole timesheet is still there: pass <code>unbilled_only: false</code> to any of them. <code>invoice_summary</code>\nreturns the <code>entry_ids</code> it used precisely so they can be handed straight to <code>entry_mark_billed</code>\nonce the invoice exists.</p>\n<p><code>report</code> and <code>invoice_summary</code> answer overlapping questions on purpose: <code>report</code> is for &quot;how much time\nand money,&quot; grouped any way you like; <code>invoice_summary</code> is for &quot;give me the lines I can put on an\ninvoice,&quot; which is a narrower, invoice-shaped view of the same entries for one project.</p>\n<h2>How it stores data</h2>\n<p>Entries, projects and rates live in one JSON file:\n<code>${XDG_DATA_HOME:-~/.local/share}/mcp-servers/time-tracker/data.json</code>.</p>\n<p>Every write (starting or stopping a timer, adding, editing or deleting an entry, setting a rate) happens\nunder an advisory lock file at <code>.../time-tracker/.lock</code>, held across the whole load-mutate-save cycle, so\ntwo overlapping calls cannot interleave and corrupt the file. The save itself writes to a temporary file\nand renames it into place, so a crash or a killed process mid-write leaves either the old file or the new\none, never a half-written one. Reads (<code>entry_list</code>, <code>report</code>, <code>timer_status</code>, <code>export_csv</code>) do not take\nthe lock.</p>\n<p>To back up your data, copy the single <code>data.json</code> file (and <code>.lock</code> if present, though it holds no data).\nThere is no database and no hidden second file.</p>\n<p>If <code>data.json</code> is ever unreadable or not valid JSON, the server does <strong>not</strong> treat that as &quot;no data yet&quot;.\nIt moves the file aside byte-for-byte as <code>data.json.corrupt-&lt;timestamp&gt;</code>, writes a <code>data.json.corrupt</code>\nmarker and makes every tool, reads included, return <code>data file is corrupt; moved to ...; nothing was written</code>. Restore a good <code>data.json</code> (the quarantined copy is right there) and delete the marker file to\ncarry on. Nothing is overwritten in the meantime.</p>\n<h2>Dates, times and rates</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Timestamps with no offset are your local time.</strong> <code>2026-09-02T09:00:00</code> means 09:00 where you are, not\nUTC. Pass an explicit offset (<code>2026-09-02T09:00:00+02:00</code>) or a trailing <code>Z</code> and it is honoured exactly.</li>\n<li><strong>Date-only bounds cover whole local days.</strong> <code>from: &quot;2026-09-01&quot;</code> is 00:00:00 local on the 1st and\n<code>to: &quot;2026-09-30&quot;</code> is 23:59:59.999 local on the 30th, so a month reported by dates includes its last\nday. Timestamps with a time are used as given.</li>\n<li><strong>Entries are clipped to the window.</strong> An entry that starts before <code>from</code> or ends after <code>to</code> counts for\nthe part inside the period, not all of it and not none of it.</li>\n<li><strong>Entries are split at local midnight for day grouping.</strong> Work from 23:30 to 01:30 is 0.5 h on the first\nday and 1.5 h on the next, including across a month boundary. <code>timer_status</code> counts only the part of an\nentry, or of the running timer, that falls after midnight today.</li>\n<li><strong>Rate strings are parsed, never guessed.</strong> <code>&quot;1,200 USD&quot;</code> is 1200 (a comma followed by exactly three\ndigits is thousands grouping), <code>&quot;12,50 EUR&quot;</code> is 12.50 (the unambiguous European decimal shape), and\n<code>&quot;1.200,50&quot;</code> is 1200.50. Anything that could mean either thing, such as <code>&quot;1,2345&quot;</code>, is refused with a\nworked example instead of being read as the wrong number.</li>\n<li><strong>Rates are captured when the time is logged.</strong> <code>entry_add</code> and <code>timer_stop</code> store the effective hourly\nrate and currency on the entry, and reports and invoices use that stored rate. <code>project_set_rate</code>\ntherefore applies to future entries only; pass <code>apply_to_existing: true</code> to re-rate the time already\nlogged for that project. That re-stamps EVERY entry of the project, including entries that already\ncarry a rate, and the response says how many changed and the project&#39;s new total. Add\n<code>only_missing: true</code> to touch only entries that captured no rate of their own.</li>\n<li><strong>Tag rows overlap.</strong> In <code>group_by: &quot;tag&quot;</code> an entry tagged <code>dev</code> and <code>meeting</code> appears in both rows; the\ntotal is computed from the entries once, so it is never the sum of the rows.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Limits and honest caveats</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Free <code>entry_list</code>, <code>report</code>, <code>export_csv</code> and <code>invoice_summary</code> only see the last 7 days. Timers and\nentries themselves are unlimited and nothing is ever deleted, the window just narrows what a free\ncall can read back.</li>\n<li>Free tier supports hourly rates on 2 projects; a third rated project needs Pro.</li>\n<li>Every <code>report</code> grouping is free, tag included: the tag total is a correctness fix, not a premium\nfeature. <code>group_by</code> itself is optional, omit it for the plain total per currency.</li>\n<li>Only one timer can run at a time. Starting a second one stops and logs the first, there is no\nconcurrent-timer mode.</li>\n<li>There is no reminder or idle-detection: if you forget to stop a timer, it keeps running until you stop\nit or start another.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Troubleshooting</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>npx</code> hangs or fails to find the package</strong>: npm publish for this package is pending. Use the <code>.mcpb</code>\nbundle or the clone-and-build path above until it lands.</li>\n<li><strong>Using the <code>.mcpb</code> bundle</strong>: it installs into Claude Desktop directly; there is no separate path to\nconfigure.</li>\n<li><strong>Using the clone path</strong>: the server binary is <code>servers/time-tracker/dist/index.js</code> after\n<code>npm run build</code>. Point your client&#39;s <code>command</code> at <code>node</code> with that absolute path as the only argument.</li>\n<li><strong>Node version</strong>: requires Node &gt;= 18. Check with <code>node -v</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Nothing shows up / silent failures</strong>: this server writes logs to stderr only, never stdout (stdout is\nreserved for the MCP protocol). In Claude Desktop, check Settings -&gt; Developer -&gt; the server&#39;s log\nfile; in Claude Code, run with <code>--mcp-debug</code> or check the terminal you launched it from.</li>\n<li><strong>A Pro key isn&#39;t recognized</strong>: run <code>license_status</code> to see what the server thinks your tier is, and\nconfirm <code>MCP_LICENSE_KEY</code> is set in the same process the client launches (not just your shell).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Privacy</h2>\n<p>All data stays local: entries live in <code>${XDG_DATA_HOME:-~/.local/share}/mcp-servers/time-tracker/data.json</code>.\nThe server makes no network requests, has no telemetry, and needs no account. License keys are Ed25519\nsignatures verified offline against a public key compiled into the package, activation works with no\ninternet connection.</p>\n<h2>Pairs with</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href="../invoice/README.md">mcp-invoice</a>, turn <code>invoice_summary</code> output straight into a numbered PDF invoice.</li>\n<li><a href="../spreadsheet/README.md">mcp-spreadsheet</a>, export a CSV with <code>export_csv</code> and query or reshape it.</li>\n<li><a href="../price-tracker/README.md">mcp-price-tracker</a>, if you also buy things for the client, watch those prices.</li>\n<li><a href="../office-suite/README.md">office-suite</a>, every sibling server behind one install, one config entry.</li>\n<li>Guide: <a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/guides/track-time-in-claude-code">Track billable hours in Claude Code and Cursor</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>FAQ</h2>\n<p>Yes. All three speak MCP over stdio with the same config shape; the tools and the data file are identical\nregardless of client.</p>\n<p>Nothing is deleted. The entry stays in <code>data.json</code> forever; it just will not appear in <code>entry_list</code>,\n<code>report</code>, <code>export_csv</code> or <code>invoice_summary</code> results until you activate Pro, which opens full history.</p>\n<p>Yes. Currency is set per project (or per entry, overriding the project default) and every total is grouped\nby currency, a report never adds EUR and USD together.</p>\n<p>The server does not block overlaps; it logs what you tell it. <code>entry_edit</code> lets you fix a mistake after\nthe fact.</p>\n<p>No. There are no network calls anywhere in this server, including for license activation, which is\nverified with a local public key.</p>\n<h2>License</h2>\n<p>MIT</p>\n<h2>One business profile for the whole suite</h2>\n<p>Your identity is stored once, at <code>${XDG_DATA_HOME:-~/.local/share}/mcp-servers/profile/business.json</code>,\nand every server in the suite reads it: the invoice issuer, the docx letterhead, the recurring\nissuer, expense-tracker&#39;s default VAT rate, time-tracker&#39;s and timezone&#39;s home zone, and the\nresume and contract letterheads. Set it once with <code>business_set</code> (invoice or docx) - you never\nrepeat it anywhere else. An email address is only ever taken from that profile or from an explicit\nargument; when none is stored, documents show <code>[add: email]</code> and the tool says so rather than\nletting anyone improvise an address.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Is there a free MCP time tracking server?</h3>\n<p>Yes. The time-tracker server at mcp.zovo.one is a free MCP time tracking server with no install: start and stop timers in chat, keep per-client totals, and produce weekly reports. Unlike SaaS trackers (WebWork, TrackingTime) it needs no account - paste the hosted URL and go.</p>\n<h3>How do I track time from Claude?</h3>\n<p>Connect <a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/mcp/time-tracker">https://mcp.zovo.one/mcp/time-tracker</a> (tokenized URL from mcp.zovo.one/mcp/connect) and say: &#39;Start a timer for the Acme project.&#39; Stop it later the same way; weekly summaries are one ask away.</p>\n<h2>Use these docs as an MCP server</h2>\n<p>Any MCP client (Claude, Cursor, Windsurf, VS Code) can read this repository&#39;s documentation directly via GitMCP - no install:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Docs MCP URL: <a href="https://gitmcp.io/theluckystrike/mcp-time-tracker">https://gitmcp.io/theluckystrike/mcp-time-tracker</a></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>First five minutes</h2>\n<p>Three prompts that scored 3 of 3, measured in round 5, 2026-09-03. Paste one in as it is written.</p>\n<pre class="prompt">I forgot to track yesterday: I worked 9 to 12:30 on the Nova redesign and 2 to 5 on Nova bug fixes, my rate for Nova is 85 USD.</pre>\n<p class="muted">Paste this into Claude with the server connected.</p>\n<p class="muted">What it did, measured in round 5, 2026-09-03: entry_add x2 with explicit start/end/rate/currency.</p>\n<pre class="prompt">Show me a standup summary for yesterday and today.</pre>\n<p class="muted">Paste this into Claude with the server connected.</p>\n<p class="muted">What it did, measured in round 5, 2026-09-03: entry_list {from 2026-09-02T00:00:00, to 2026-09-03T23:59:59}.</p>\n<pre class="prompt">Export my Nova time as CSV for last month.</pre>\n<p class="muted">Paste this into Claude with the server connected.</p>\n<p class="muted">What it did, measured in round 5, 2026-09-03: export_csv {from 2026-08-01, to 2026-08-31, project: Nova} -&gt; \'Wrote 0 entries\' plus the free-tier note.</p>\n<p class="muted">On the free tier for this path: Unlimited timers and entries; reports, lists and CSV cover the last 7 days; 2 projects with rates. Currency per entry (EUR, USD, GBP, PLN).</p>\n\n<p>Or <a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/buy/bundle?src=store.s.time-tracker.bundle">the 49-server bundle for $39</a>.</p>', "first_five": [{ "round": 5, "date": "2026-09-03", "file": "user_value_r5.json", "id": "1", "score": 3, "prompt": "I forgot to track yesterday: I worked 9 to 12:30 on the Nova redesign and 2 to 5 on Nova bug fixes, my rate for Nova is 85 USD.", "evidence": "entry_add x2 with explicit start/end/rate/currency." }, { "round": 5, "date": "2026-09-03", "file": "user_value_r5.json", "id": "2", "score": 3, "prompt": "Show me a standup summary for yesterday and today.", "evidence": "entry_list {from 2026-09-02T00:00:00, to 2026-09-03T23:59:59}." }, { "round": 5, "date": "2026-09-03", "file": "user_value_r5.json", "id": "9", "score": 3, "prompt": "Export my Nova time as CSV for last month.", "evidence": "export_csv {from 2026-08-01, to 2026-08-31, project: Nova} -> 'Wrote 0 entries' plus the free-tier note." }], "first_five_round": 5, "first_five_date": "2026-09-03" }, "price-tracker": { "title": "MCP Price Tracker", "tagline": "Check and watch product prices on ordinary shop pages.", "description": "Fetches a product page and extracts the price from JSON-LD, microdata, Open Graph tags, common price markup or a currency-aware text fallback, normalising 1.299,00 EUR and 1,299.00 USD alike. Stores watches with a price history, refreshes them, computes min, max and change, and flags target hits and drops of 5% or more. Manual entry covers pages that block bots.", "html": `<h1>Check and watch product prices on ordinary shop pages.</h1>
<p><strong>Featured on <a href="mcpservers.org">Awesome MCP Servers</a></strong> - <a href="https://mcpservers.org/servers/github-com-theluckystrike-mcp-servers-tree-main-servers-price-tracker">directory listing</a> | <a href="https://mcp.zovo.one/s/price-tracker">live hosted endpoint</a>, free tier, no signup.</p>
<p>Ask your assistant what something costs right now. Point it at any product page and it reads the price off the page, remembers it, and tells you next time whether the price moved, how far, and whether it reached the number you were waiting for. It works on ordinary shop pages by reading the structured data most stores already publish (JSON-LD, Open Graph, microdata) and falling back to the visible price when they do not. When a large retailer blocks automated requests it says so plainly and lets you record the price yourself so the history stays intact. Everything is stored in a JSON file on your own machine.</p>
<p>Built by <a href="https://github.com/theluckystrike">theluckystrike</a>.</p>
<p><strong>In the <a href="https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io/v0.1/servers/io.github.theluckystrike%2Fprice-tracker-drop-alert-watch/versions/latest">official MCP Registry</a></strong> (<code>io.github.theluckystrike/price-tracker-drop-alert-watch</code>).</p>
<p><img src="../../assets/demo-price-tracker.gif" alt="price-tracker demo"></p>
<p>Watch any product page for price drops from chat, no scraping service, no account, all local.</p>
<h2>60-second install</h2>
<p>npm publish for <code>@theluckystrike/mcp-price-tracker</code> is pending. Until then, the <code>.mcpb</code> one-click bundle or a clone+build
is the working path, both are verified below.</p>
<p>One-click (.mcpb): download <code>price-tracker.mcpb</code> from the latest release and double-click it in Claude Desktop:
<a href="https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-servers/releases/latest">https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-servers/releases/latest</a></p>
<p>(<code>claude_desktop_config.json</code>):</p>
<pre><code class="language-json">{
  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {
    &quot;price-tracker&quot;: {
      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,
      &quot;args&quot;: [&quot;-y&quot;, &quot;@theluckystrike/mcp-price-tracker&quot;]
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Claude Code:</p>
<pre><code class="language-sh">claude mcp add price-tracker -- npx -y @theluckystrike/mcp-price-tracker
</code></pre>
<p>(<code>.cursor/mcp.json</code>):</p>
<pre><code class="language-json">{
  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {
    &quot;price-tracker&quot;: {
      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,
      &quot;args&quot;: [&quot;-y&quot;, &quot;@theluckystrike/mcp-price-tracker&quot;]
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>npx</code> form above starts working the moment the package is published. Until then, use the .mcpb bundle above, or
build from source with exactly these three commands:</p>
<pre><code class="language-sh">git clone https://github.com/theluckystrike/mcp-servers.git &amp;&amp; cd mcp-servers
npm install
npm run build -w packages/mcp-license -w servers/price-tracker
</code></pre>
<p>Then point your client&#39;s <code>command</code> at <code>node</code> with one arg: the absolute path to <code>servers/price-tracker/dist/index.js</code>.</p>
<p>To run in Pro mode set <code>MCP_LICENSE_KEY</code> in the same config block, or call <code>license_activate</code> once with your key.</p>
<h2>Tools</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Tool</th>
<th>What it does</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td><code>price_check</code></td>
<td>Fetch a page now; returns price, currency, title, extraction confidence and the change against the last stored observation for that URL. No watch needed.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>watch_add</code></td>
<td>Start tracking a URL. Stores the first observation. Optional <code>label</code>, <code>target_price</code>, <code>currency</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>watch_list</code></td>
<td>Every watch with current price, min, max, change, target, confidence and source.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>watch_remove</code></td>
<td>Stop tracking, by <code>id</code> or <code>url</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>watch_refresh</code></td>
<td>Re-fetch one watch (<code>id</code>) or all of them (<code>all: true</code>); appends observations and returns current, previous, min, max, change % and target-hit flags. This is the only thing that checks prices.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>price_history</code></td>
<td>Stored observations for one watch, oldest first, optional <code>limit</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>price_add_manual</code></td>
<td>Record a price you read yourself, for shops that block bots. Creates the watch if needed.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>alerts_pending</code></td>
<td>Watches whose latest price is at or below target, or which dropped 5% or more since the previous observation. Free.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>license_status</code></td>
<td>Free or Pro, and where to upgrade.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>license_activate</code></td>
<td>Activate a Pro key. Verified offline.</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Resource: <code>prices://watches</code> - JSON of every watch with its latest price.</p>
<p>Prompt: <code>check_prices</code> - refreshes every watch and summarises the drops and target hits in one command.</p>
<h2>What you can say</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>You say</th>
<th>Tool</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>&quot;What does this cost right now: &lt;url&gt;?&quot;</td>
<td><code>price_check</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;Watch that page and alert me if it goes under 40.&quot;</td>
<td><code>watch_add</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;Show me everything I&#39;m watching and whether anything dropped.&quot;</td>
<td><code>watch_refresh</code> (all) then <code>watch_list</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;Stop tracking that laptop, I bought it elsewhere.&quot;</td>
<td><code>watch_remove</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;Refresh my watches.&quot;</td>
<td><code>watch_refresh</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;What&#39;s the price history on the bookshelf I&#39;m watching?&quot;</td>
<td><code>price_history</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;I checked myself, it&#39;s 39.99 GBP in-store, log that.&quot;</td>
<td><code>price_add_manual</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;Has anything I&#39;m watching hit its target or dropped a lot?&quot;</td>
<td><code>alerts_pending</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;Am I on the free plan or Pro?&quot;</td>
<td><code>license_status</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>&quot;Here&#39;s my license key, activate it.&quot;</td>
<td><code>license_activate</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Worked example</h2>
<p>A real run against <code>books.toscrape.com</code>, from <code>docs/USER_VALUE_R2.md</code>, with <code>WebFetch</code>/<code>WebSearch</code>
disallowed so the price-tracker&#39;s own tools had to answer:</p>
<pre><code>You: What does this cost right now: https://books.toscrape.com/catalogue/...?

  price_check { url: &quot;https://books.toscrape.com/catalogue/...&quot; }
  -&gt; price 51.77, currency GBP, source regex-fallback, confidence low

Assistant: GBP 51.77. Confidence: low (regex-fallback) -- this page has no
structured price data, so I read it from the visible text.
</code></pre>
<p>The confidence label is not decoration: <code>books.toscrape.com</code> carries no JSON-LD or microdata, so this is
the one case in testing where the regex fallback was the only path to a number, and the model relayed the
&quot;low&quot; label to the user unprompted rather than stating 51.77 as a fact.</p>
<h2>Alerts: nothing runs in the background</h2>
<p>There is no scheduler and no daemon. A watch is a stored history, not a subscription: prices are re-read only when <code>watch_refresh</code> runs, and <code>alerts_pending</code> reports on what is already stored. The working pattern is to say <strong>&quot;refresh my watches&quot;</strong> at the start of a session (or run the <code>check_prices</code> prompt), then ask what dropped.</p>
